# ===========================================================================
# CardDemo :: Java migration module :: version control exclusions
# ===========================================================================
#
# SCOPE
# ~~~~~
# This file is deliberately MODULE SCOPED. It sits at the root of the Java
# module and every pattern below is therefore resolved relative to this
# directory. Nothing here can reach the legacy mainframe estate that lives
# beside this module: that estate is the byte-for-byte parity baseline and
# the anchor of the paragraph level traceability matrix, so it must remain
# untouched. No pattern in this file uses a parent directory traversal or an
# absolute path, and none ever should.
#
# THE FAILURE MODE THIS FILE GUARDS AGAINST
# ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
# The dangerous defect in an ignore file is not the artifact it forgets to
# exclude - a stray build output is noticed the first time somebody reviews a
# diff. The dangerous defect is the artifact it wrongly excludes, because a
# required file that silently stops being tracked is invisible until a clean
# checkout fails to build. Four broad patterns are therefore FORBIDDEN here,
# each because it would swallow a required deliverable:
#
#   *.properties         would swallow .mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.properties
#                        and break the hermetic wrapper driven build for
#                        every other developer
#   application-*.yml    would swallow all four committed Spring profile
#                        documents under src/main/resources
#   *.txt                would swallow the fixed width golden fixtures the
#                        byte parity assertions compare against, and the
#                        startup banner
#   *.json               would swallow the externalised validation lookup
#                        tables and the provisioned metrics dashboard
#
# Patterns are consequently written as narrowly as the artifact allows, and
# anchored with a leading slash whenever the artifact only ever appears at
# the module root. The closing section of this file re-includes the highest
# risk deliverables explicitly: git applies the LAST matching pattern, so
# those negations also act as a tripwire against a future broad addition.
#
# CASING
# ~~~~~~
# Extension globs are written as lowercase and UPPERCASE pairs rather than
# lowercase only, following the same case inclusive pairing convention the
# migration plan applies to the legacy estate, whose members mix casing.
# Fixed file names that a tool resolves only in lowercase - the Spring
# profile resources and the Maven plugin residue below - are matched exactly
# as those tools emit them, because an uppercase twin cannot occur.
#
# ===========================================================================
# 1. MAVEN BUILD OUTPUT
# ===========================================================================
# Everything the build produces. Anchored, because Maven writes its output
# directory at the module root only; an unanchored pattern would also match
# any future source or resource directory that happened to share the name.
/target/

# Defensive. Gradle is not the build tool for this module, and IDE driven
# compilation writes elsewhere, but neither directory is ever a source.
/build/
/out/
/bin/

# The Maven Wrapper is distributed as a script only, so no wrapper jar is
# committed. Excluding the jar keeps an unscanned binary out of the
# repository if a developer's tooling regenerates one.
#
# NOTE the wrapper DIRECTORY is deliberately not excluded. Git cannot
# re-include a file that sits beneath an excluded directory, so excluding
# .mvn/ or .mvn/wrapper/ would make the maven-wrapper.properties negation at
# the foot of this file inert and would silently break the hermetic build.
.mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.jar
.mvn/.develocity/
.mvn/.gradle-enterprise/

# Residue left behind by Maven release, versions, shade, flatten and
# buildnumber plugins. Each is a complete file name, so none of them can
# match the wrapper properties file.
dependency-reduced-pom.xml
release.properties
pom.xml.releaseBackup
pom.xml.versionsBackup
pom.xml.next
pom.xml.tag
buildNumber.properties
.flattened-pom.xml

# Compiled classes, wherever a tool decides to drop them.
*.class
*.CLASS

# ===========================================================================
# 2. VALIDATION GATE REPORT OUTPUT
# ===========================================================================
# The build generates the evidence for the coverage gate and the supply
# chain gate. That evidence is transcribed into the gate evidence document
# as a recorded result; the raw report output is reproducible and must never
# be committed, so that a clean checkout plus one build is the only way to
# obtain it and no stale report can be mistaken for a current one.
#
# Coverage execution data and the generated report trees are written beneath
# the build directory and are already covered by /target/ above. They are
# named again here because both locations are overridable from the command
# line and would then land outside it.
*.exec
*.EXEC
surefire-reports/
failsafe-reports/
/target/test-output/

# Vulnerability scan reports. The scan is bound to the verify phase, emits
# HTML, JSON and XML, and defaults into the build directory.
dependency-check-report.*

# Local copy of the vulnerability database. Large, entirely regenerable and
# not a source artifact. The build resolves this beneath the user's home
# directory by default; these patterns cover the case where it is redirected
# into the module instead.
.dependency-check/
dependency-check-data/

# ===========================================================================
# 3. LOCAL SECRETS AND CREDENTIAL MATERIAL
# ===========================================================================
# The binding constraint on this module is that no credential is hardcoded:
# every secret resolves from the environment with no fallback default, so a
# missing secret fails startup rather than silently binding a placeholder.
# An ignore file cannot enforce that, but it is the last line of defence
# against a local secret reaching version control.
#
# This section is load bearing rather than ceremonial. The container compose
# definition interpolates the AWS access key, the AWS secret key and the
# database password from the environment, and Compose reads a .env file from
# this very directory - which makes a module level .env the single most
# likely place for a real credential to be committed by accident.
#
# Ordering matters: git applies the last matching pattern, so the template
# re-inclusion must follow the two exclusions.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Private keys, certificates and keystores. No such artifact is a source
# file in this module, so excluding the extensions outright is safe.
*.pem
*.PEM
*.key
*.KEY
*.p12
*.P12
*.pfx
*.PFX
*.jks
*.JKS
*.keystore
*.KEYSTORE
*.truststore
*.TRUSTSTORE
secrets/

# Developer configuration overrides, which commonly carry real credentials.
#
# NOTE none of the five patterns below can match a committed profile
# document. application-secret*.yml requires the literal segment "secret"
# after the hyphen, and application-*.local.yml requires a ".local.yml"
# suffix that application-local.yml does not have. The module resolves its
# own profiles from YAML throughout, so the properties form on the first
# line is a local override by definition.
application-local.properties
application-secret*.yml
application-secret*.yaml
application-*.local.yml
application-*.local.yaml

# Cloud credential files. Anchored to the module root on purpose: an
# unanchored "credentials" pattern would also match a directory of that name
# anywhere in the source tree, which is an entirely plausible package name.
/credentials
/credentials.*
/aws.properties
.aws/

# ===========================================================================
# 4. IDE AND EDITOR STATE
# ===========================================================================
# Project local tool state, excluded so that a pull request diff shows only
# the change being contributed, per the repository's contribution guidance.

# IntelliJ IDEA
.idea/
*.iml
*.IML
*.ipr
*.IPR
*.iws
*.IWS

# Eclipse
.classpath
.project
.settings/
.factorypath
.apt_generated/

# Visual Studio Code
.vscode/

# NetBeans. Only the private and generated subtrees, because a shared
# nbproject configuration would be a committed artifact.
nbproject/private/
nbbuild/
nbdist/
.nb-gradle/

# Editor scratch, patch and merge residue
*~
*.swp
*.SWP
*.swo
*.SWO
*.bak
*.BAK
*.orig
*.ORIG
*.rej
*.REJ

# ===========================================================================
# 5. OPERATING SYSTEM NOISE
# ===========================================================================
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini
desktop.ini

# ===========================================================================
# 6. RUNTIME LOGS AND JVM DIAGNOSTIC OUTPUT
# ===========================================================================
# The module replaces the legacy program's console display diagnostics with
# structured JSON logging. The shipped logging configuration writes to the
# console only, so these patterns are defensive: they cover a developer who
# enables a file destination locally, and the crash and profiling output the
# JVM writes of its own accord.
*.log
*.LOG
*.log.*
/logs/

# JVM fatal error, compiler replay and heap dump output. Heap dumps in
# particular appear whenever the peak memory figure for the performance
# baseline is being measured.
hs_err_pid*
replay_pid*
*.hprof
*.HPROF
heapdump*

# Core dumps. Narrowed on purpose: a bare "core.*" pattern would wrongly
# exclude an ordinary file such as core.json or core.yml, whereas a Linux
# core dump is named either "core" or "core" followed by the process id.
/core
core.[0-9]*

# ===========================================================================
# 7. LOCAL CONTAINER AND EMULATED AWS STATE
# ===========================================================================
# The local validation stack brings up PostgreSQL, the emulated AWS
# services, Prometheus, Grafana and the tracing backend. Its persistent
# state currently lives in named Docker volumes rather than host mounts, so
# nothing appears in the working tree today; these patterns cover the common
# local change of switching a named volume to a bind mount.
#
# NOTE the committed assets the stack mounts read only - the metrics scrape
# configuration and dashboard provisioning under config/, and the resource
# bootstrap script under localstack/ - are NOT excluded and must never be.
postgres-data/
localstack-data/
prometheus-data/
grafana-data/
volume/
volumes/
.localstack/

# ===========================================================================
# 8. EXPLICITLY PROTECTED DELIVERABLES
# ===========================================================================
# Required, committed artifacts, re-included by name. Git applies the last
# matching pattern, so these lines win over everything above and will also
# neutralise a future broad pattern added anywhere before them.
#
# These negations are effective because no directory pattern in this file
# excludes any of their parent directories - a negation cannot resurrect a
# file beneath an excluded directory.

# The Maven Wrapper descriptor. Losing this breaks the hermetic build.
!.mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.properties

# The four Spring profile documents. The production profile is safe to
# commit precisely because it holds no secret value, only unresolved
# environment references with no fallback default.
!src/main/resources/application.yml
!src/main/resources/application-local.yml
!src/main/resources/application-test.yml
!src/main/resources/application-prod.yml

# Startup banner and structured logging configuration.
!src/main/resources/banner.txt
!src/main/resources/logback-spring.xml

# Byte parity fixtures. The expected files are fixed width golden output at
# the legacy record widths and are the reference the parity assertions
# compare against, so they are sources rather than generated artifacts.
!src/test/resources/fixtures/input/**
!src/test/resources/fixtures/expected/**
